WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.889
It's great to welcome to the program today,
David Epstein, who's the author of the New

00:00:03.889 --> 00:00:04.900
York Times best

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:09.969
cellar range wide generalists, triumph in
a specialized world.

00:00:09.969 --> 00:00:12.929
Also the best selling book, the sports gene
as well.

00:00:12.929 --> 00:00:16.299
Today we'll be talking about generalists in
a specialized world.

00:00:16.299 --> 00:00:17.949
David, great to have you on.

00:00:17.949 --> 00:00:19.470
Thank you for having me.

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:23.869
So this is a super interesting topic to me
because if you read some of the scholarship

00:00:23.869 --> 00:00:28.380
about the future of work in the United States,
uh, and the types of jobs that are more and

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:35.530
less likely to be automated away, one of the
things that often comes up is jobs that require

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:43.050
sort of, uh, working in the gray areas, subjective
advice, interpretation of material, et Cetera,

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:46.449
are less likely to be automated away.

00:00:46.449 --> 00:00:52.930
And that very often those are the types of
jobs that are more rather than less specialized

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:57.220
that the more general tasks are easier to
automate away.

00:00:57.220 --> 00:01:02.420
But in your book you talk about actually the
value of generalist knowledge, general knowledge

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:04.470
rather than specialization in the modern world.

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:05.470
So let's start there.

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:07.540
Like why, why do you believe that that's so
important.

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:11.830
I would say that historically they actually,
the opposite has been true that it's the specialist

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:13.979
jobs to get audited and automated away.

00:01:13.979 --> 00:01:18.030
So one of the examples I talk about in the
book is, is chess, right?

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:20.290
Where chess is what's called a kind learning
environment.

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:24.040
It's with a psychologist, Robin Hogarth turned
to kind learning and term to kind learning

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:28.430
environment, which means it's based on repetitive
patterns, essentially information's clear.

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:32.270
Uh, the task next year will look like it did
last year.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:36.650
The experts advantage in fact is based on
static rules and the ability to recognize

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:37.650
patterns.

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:41.189
And in a scenario like that, specialization
is a, an enormous advantage.

00:01:41.189 --> 00:01:45.689
So in fact, if you haven't started studying
chess patterns by the age of 12, your chances

00:01:45.689 --> 00:01:51.560
of ever becoming an international master dropped
from about one in four to about one in 55.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:56.210
But that's also why chess is, uh, so easy
to automate because it's based on static rules

00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:57.210
and repetitive patterns.

00:01:57.210 --> 00:01:59.909
And we see similar things in other parts of
the work world.

00:01:59.909 --> 00:02:01.899
To give an example that I can sort of tell
quickly.

00:02:01.899 --> 00:02:08.790
I guess when the ATM came online, the prophecy
was that the bank teller would be a extinct

00:02:08.790 --> 00:02:09.790
overnight.

00:02:09.790 --> 00:02:14.430
But in fact, as we've had more bank tail,
more ATM in America, we've had more bank tellers

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:17.850
and that's because ATM is make each bank branch
cheaper to operate.

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:21.690
And so you need fewer tellers, but banks opened
more branches and you end up with more total

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:22.690
tellers.

00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:27.500
But it completely changes the job from one
of repetitive transactions, um, to one of

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:32.990
someone who is more of a marketing professional
and a customer service, uh, agent.

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:37.320
And they have to have this much broader range
of often much sort of softer skills.

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:41.330
And that, that actually, um, looks like the
pattern when you actually look at what happens

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:46.200
as opposed to to what people, uh, predict
and, and are not accountable for those predictions.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:47.200
Yes.

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:50.350
And much to the dismay of many people having
to be sold things every time they go in and

00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:51.640
just want to deposit a check.

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:54.410
Are you sure you don't want to, uh, open up
a new CD or whatever?

00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:56.500
Yeah, I certainly have experienced that.

00:02:56.500 --> 00:03:00.810
Um, there's a big analogy here I want to get
into the work side more, but there is a big

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:05.450
analogy here to sports that there are lots
of analogies to sports in fact of specialists

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:07.000
versus generalists.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:10.700
Can you talk a little bit with some of the
sports examples that you cite about ways to

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:15.320
think about generalist versus specialist in
some particular sports context?

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:16.320
Sure.

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:20.850
Well, I mean the, the books I'm partly came
out of a debate I was set up to have with

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:23.280
Malcolm Gladwell, right about the 10,000 hour
rule.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:24.960
It's on, it's on youtube.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:30.560
Um, and basically his hypothesis was that
early specialization in sports is an insurmountable

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:31.560
advantage.

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:34.010
And I was the science writer at sports illustrated
at the time.

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:36.390
And so I went and looked at what research
had to say.

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:40.670
And in fact, in most sports, when scientists
look at athletes who go on to become elite,

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:44.190
they find that they have what's called a sampling
period where they learn this broader range

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:46.510
of skills that scaffold later learning.

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:48.720
Uh, they, they play a variety of sports.

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:51.990
They learn about their interests and abilities
and they systematically delay specializing

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:54.640
until later than their peers who plateau at
lower levels.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:57.000
Now, there are some exceptions to that.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.240
Um, golf I think is one exception to that
and that's why I opened the book.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:03.730
Comparing and contrasting a tiger woods and
Roger Federer.

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:05.450
Golf is the epitome of a kind learning environment.

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:07.540
Again, all the information is clear.

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:09.410
You're, it's almost like an industrial task.

00:04:09.410 --> 00:04:13.750
You're trying to do the same known movements
over and over with as little deviation as

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:18.780
possible, whereas in the sports that are more
dynamic, which are sort of more a little bit

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:22.490
more toward the end of what Hogarth would
call a wicked learning environment where you

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:25.160
can't just do the same thing over and over
essentially.

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:31.080
Um, in those sports, you almost, you pretty
much ubiquitously see this, this sampling

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:36.540
period, this, this broad broad range of skills
gained early and delayed specialization and

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:41.460
is in fact like one of the hallmarks of, of,
uh, the path to expertise and those other,

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:42.870
yeah, I guess it's easy to imagine.

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:43.870
Well.

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:51.720
So NHL goalies today are highly, highly specialized,
but in many cases they only became special

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:56.650
specialists in being goalies after they had
already become just generally good hockey

00:04:56.650 --> 00:05:01.130
players in their communities in high school,
maybe even in college to some degree.

00:05:01.130 --> 00:05:02.130
Yeah.

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:06.830
And even actually if you look at, so I, I
filled a full page of small of, of shrunken

00:05:06.830 --> 00:05:12.300
size type in the back of the book with citations
showing all, you know, not all, but many of

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:15.130
the studies have delayed specialization athletes
who want to become elite.

00:05:15.130 --> 00:05:20.470
And one of them is in fact of current NFL
players who did more unstructured play and

00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:24.730
sampled more sports formally or unformally
than peers who plateaued at lower levels.

00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:29.640
So, um, you know, sports from volleyball and
baseball, football, soccer, hockey, those

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:30.640
citations.

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:33.332
I stuck all back there because I thought people
would be interested in being able to look

00:05:33.332 --> 00:05:36.230
up whatever sport, whatever their favorite
sport was basically.

00:05:36.230 --> 00:05:40.010
So to bring this back to careers and thinking
about jobs, I mean there are lots of careers

00:05:40.010 --> 00:05:45.070
where there is this natural flow of being
a generalist and then specializing.

00:05:45.070 --> 00:05:49.220
I mean everybody starts other than I guess
dentists and podiatrists, maybe there's some

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:51.440
other exotic exceptions.

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:55.740
Everybody starts going to medical school,
whether you end up in psychiatry or thoracic

00:05:55.740 --> 00:05:59.680
surgery, and then there is a sort of natural
flow at which you start to specialize.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:04.570
Sometimes it relates to the residency that
a period that you have in the law, people

00:06:04.570 --> 00:06:10.000
start with a very similar first year law curriculum
and then eventually you start to specialize

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:12.110
sometimes through an internship.

00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:19.620
Is there a sort of general rule about the
transition between being a generalist into

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:22.670
a specialist that applies more broadly?

00:06:22.670 --> 00:06:24.220
So think it sort of depends, right?

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:27.639
Medical School, I guess your more general
in medical school than when you become a hyper

00:06:27.639 --> 00:06:28.639
specialist later.

00:06:28.639 --> 00:06:29.639
Right.

00:06:29.639 --> 00:06:33.639
And a hyper specialist now like a cardiologist
used to be the epitome of specialization in

00:06:33.639 --> 00:06:34.639
medicine.

00:06:34.639 --> 00:06:38.290
Now to be specialized, a cardiologist might
be someone who only works on cardiac valves,

00:06:38.290 --> 00:06:42.800
you know the little doors that, that let blood
in now to the heart and even the rest of the

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:44.830
heart is outside of their purview.

00:06:44.830 --> 00:06:50.260
And that's caused, um, a major problem of
overtreatment with things that don't work

00:06:50.260 --> 00:06:53.580
because all these specialists are looking
at what's called surrogate markers.

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:56.690
Basically they're looking at some small part
of a system and assuming that it's a proxy

00:06:56.690 --> 00:06:59.150
for the rest of the system and it turns out
that it often isn't.

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:03.350
So you know, we'll have things like very popular
blood pressure medications and people who

00:07:03.350 --> 00:07:06.880
take it die of heart attack and stroke at
the exact same rates, um, just with lower

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:08.200
blood pressure numbers.

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:12.070
So I think while the beginning of med school
is certainly less specialized than someone

00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:15.970
becomes eventually, I think there's a movement,
and this is, this is one of the pioneers of

00:07:15.970 --> 00:07:19.889
this I profile in the last chapter of the
book who is saying, look, we need to stop.

00:07:19.889 --> 00:07:22.680
We need to stop beginning med school with
reductionism.

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:27.460
Essentially it's anatomy and physiology before
people learn how to think about a whole organism

00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:28.540
in context.

00:07:28.540 --> 00:07:31.590
And so already in the beginning of some of
those schools, I think there's a pretty, pretty

00:07:31.590 --> 00:07:35.940
good argument to be made that it's starting
with the type of specialization that's, that's

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:40.490
sort of counterproductive in terms of which
direction people go, whether they specialize

00:07:40.490 --> 00:07:41.630
or generalized first.

00:07:41.630 --> 00:07:44.639
Uh, in sports it's clear that you should generalize
first.

00:07:44.639 --> 00:07:49.620
Um, in some other areas, like when I look
at technological innovation, the biggest impact,

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:53.330
um, there are generalists and specialists
and they are defined in that type of research

00:07:53.330 --> 00:07:57.880
based on how many different types of technologies
they work across as categorized by the patent

00:07:57.880 --> 00:08:02.100
office and the specialists who, you know,
stick to one or two make contributions to

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:06.310
generalists who go across a whole bunch of
technological classes, make contributions

00:08:06.310 --> 00:08:10.280
at dilettantes who don't go very deep or very
broad, don't make big contributions, but the

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:15.190
biggest ones come from the polymaths who either
start with an anchored in area of expertise

00:08:15.190 --> 00:08:19.900
and then sacrifice some depth for breadth
or start broad and then sacrifice going broader

00:08:19.900 --> 00:08:20.900
for some depth.

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:24.490
So it doesn't really matter what direction
they take, uh, as long as they, as long as

00:08:24.490 --> 00:08:28.510
they ultimately get the breadth that is the
ally rather than the enemy of depth.

00:08:28.510 --> 00:08:33.270
So in a really general sense, it would seem
as though in a world where there are at least

00:08:33.270 --> 00:08:39.090
questions about how employment is going to
work in 20 years or in 30 years or whatever,

00:08:39.090 --> 00:08:44.130
that there would be some kind of obvious superficial
advantage to having more general knowledge

00:08:44.130 --> 00:08:48.140
and abilities because of the flexibility that
it would conceivably provide to allow someone

00:08:48.140 --> 00:08:53.420
to switch career or, or industry more easily
than someone who is a specialist or a hyper

00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:54.420
specialist.

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:59.460
Is that something that people now going into
college and choosing majors should be thinking

00:08:59.460 --> 00:09:02.760
of or, or is that a what appears to be common
sense?

00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:05.170
Not actually that that much common sense?

00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:09.700
No, I, I, I, I do think they should be thinking
about that and I don't even think it requires

00:09:09.700 --> 00:09:14.890
changing careers necessarily because the even
jobs themselves are going to change.

00:09:14.890 --> 00:09:18.820
So even if you stick with the same job or
career, you're going to need to evolve pretty

00:09:18.820 --> 00:09:19.820
quickly.

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:24.510
So the scholar James Besson, who, um, did
an amazing job of documenting sort of technological

00:09:24.510 --> 00:09:29.210
disruption over time, uh, his, one of his
findings is that we're not really going through

00:09:29.210 --> 00:09:33.360
an unprecedented time in terms of the scale
of disruption has happened many times before.

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:36.460
It's just that workers may have to reinvent
themselves more times than they did in the

00:09:36.460 --> 00:09:39.380
past because of the pace of the change.

00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:42.200
And so I think that's, that's pretty much
going to be true for everyone.

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:45.400
Starting with the late baby boomers, actually,
the bureau of Labor Statistics data shows

00:09:45.400 --> 00:09:51.120
that starting with late baby boomers, the
average number of jobs from age 18 to about

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:52.580
50 is 12.

00:09:52.580 --> 00:09:56.400
And so this really, this, this idea of the
itinerant millennial sort of actually started

00:09:56.400 --> 00:09:59.330
with the very last of the baby boomers and
it's just accelerating now.

00:09:59.330 --> 00:10:02.120
So I think we should expect to be having to
reinvent.

00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:06.180
And in fact, if you look at it, so one of
the studies I look at in range is done by

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:07.180
an economist.

00:10:07.180 --> 00:10:12.000
Looking at whether these early or late specialized
hours, um, who wins the trade off in college,

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:13.000
right?

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:17.040
The people who get into their specialty right
away or those who try a variety of things

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:20.760
and delay sampling and, and sorry in sample
and delay specializing.

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:25.490
And what he finds is that the early specializes
do jump out to an income lead because they

00:10:25.490 --> 00:10:31.120
have more domain specific skills, but by year
six, the later specializes shoot pass them.

00:10:31.120 --> 00:10:34.430
And then the early specializes have to start
quitting their entire career track in much

00:10:34.430 --> 00:10:37.160
higher numbers because they don't have that
flexibility to adapt.

00:10:37.160 --> 00:10:40.210
Now when they do quit, it's often to improve
their match quality.

00:10:40.210 --> 00:10:41.210
They find a better fit.

00:10:41.210 --> 00:10:42.800
So there their growth rates are pretty good.

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:44.970
So they should be quitting when they do that.

00:10:44.970 --> 00:10:48.580
But you know, they, they lose the tradeoff
in pretty short order.

00:10:48.580 --> 00:10:52.200
They're made to choose so early that they
often make bad choices for what they should

00:10:52.200 --> 00:10:53.200
be doing.

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:54.370
And they also don't end up as flexible.

00:10:54.370 --> 00:10:57.270
So the late specialized years, um, lose a
bit in the short term.

00:10:57.270 --> 00:10:58.430
But when in the longterm,

00:10:58.430 --> 00:11:02.300
if someone's in the middle of their career
right now, in other words, they, they're not

00:11:02.300 --> 00:11:06.430
close enough to retirement that this is not
that big of an issue for them.

00:11:06.430 --> 00:11:09.430
It's also not early enough that they're going
to be thinking about it in the way that a

00:11:09.430 --> 00:11:14.000
college student is going to be thinking about
what are the priorities and sort of most important

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:16.070
things to be doing at the mid career level?

00:11:16.070 --> 00:11:18.509
Yeah, I think some of it depends on their
goals, right?

00:11:18.509 --> 00:11:21.790
So let's say they want to be become an executive
because if they're in their mid career, that

00:11:21.790 --> 00:11:24.089
may be something that they're, that they're
targeting.

00:11:24.089 --> 00:11:28.300
So recent research by Linkedin on a half million
members found that one of the best predictors

00:11:28.300 --> 00:11:31.680
of who would go on to become an executive
was the number of different job functions

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:33.870
that someone had worked across in an industry.

00:11:33.870 --> 00:11:37.940
So if they're sort of settled on their industry,
you know, the best advice, well this is not

00:11:37.940 --> 00:11:41.810
my advice, it's from the chief economist of
Linkedin, is to work across a large number

00:11:41.810 --> 00:11:42.899
of job functions.

00:11:42.899 --> 00:11:47.500
Each additional job function saves about three
years on average of experience in terms of

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:49.550
trying to, trying to get to the c suite.

00:11:49.550 --> 00:11:55.029
I think if they're more oriented toward, um,
you know, I, I think a way to focus, well,

00:11:55.029 --> 00:11:57.970
something else to remember by the way is that
we always think of, I was at a motley fool

00:11:57.970 --> 00:12:01.400
event recently and they took something from
the introduction of my book to pull the crowd

00:12:01.400 --> 00:12:07.690
where they asked what do you think is the
average age of, um, a founder of a blockbuster

00:12:07.690 --> 00:12:11.750
startup on the day it was founded, not when
it becomes a blockbuster 25, 35, 45 or 55

00:12:11.750 --> 00:12:14.200
25 was the overwhelming favorite.

00:12:14.200 --> 00:12:16.860
And then it went down to 35 and then 45 and
55.

00:12:16.860 --> 00:12:21.250
And the answer based on research by MIT in
the Census Bureau is 45 and a half.

00:12:21.250 --> 00:12:25.470
We just, you know, just like, just like we've
heard the tiger woods story of early specialization

00:12:25.470 --> 00:12:28.529
but not the Roger Federer story where he played
a dozen different sports.

00:12:28.529 --> 00:12:29.810
We hear the Mark Zuckerberg story.

00:12:29.810 --> 00:12:33.590
You know, when at 22 he said young people
are just smarter, but we don't know the norm,

00:12:33.590 --> 00:12:38.180
which is that 45 disease people have to zigzag
a little bit before they know what to do.

00:12:38.180 --> 00:12:41.600
So one, I think that's prime time to be thinking
about starting your own thing, if that's something

00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:42.600
of interest to you.

00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:48.730
If it's not, I think the principle to remember
is I'm seeking what economists called match

00:12:48.730 --> 00:12:51.910
quality, which is a degree of fit between
your interests, your abilities, and the work

00:12:51.910 --> 00:12:52.910
that you do.

00:12:52.910 --> 00:12:57.610
And the better your match quality, the harder
you work, the more resilient you'll be.

00:12:57.610 --> 00:13:01.320
As, as one of the researchers I quote says,
when you get fit, it will look like grit.

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:04.800
That means when people get into a good spot
for themselves, um, they'll display all the

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:07.800
characteristics of grit anyway, like resilience
and work ethic.

00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:11.820
So don't stop trying to triangulate your best
fit, run lots of short term experiments.

00:13:11.820 --> 00:13:15.470
And this, this advice is kind of laid out
in more detail in the book where you keep

00:13:15.470 --> 00:13:18.459
making sure to try something new instead of
just doing what you've done so that you can

